<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Griqualand West Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1873 CE – 1880 CE · capital Kimberley</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived British Crown colony around the Kimberley diamond fields, the cradle of South Africa's mining industry.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the 1871 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keate-award">Keate Award</a> upheld <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaas-waterboer">Nicolaas Waterboer's</a> claim to the diamond diggings of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/griqualand-west">Griqualand West</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> annexed the area. It was governed at first through the Cape and was proclaimed a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> in 1873, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-southey">Richard Southey</a> as lieutenant-governor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>, the town around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/big-hole">Big Hole</a>, as its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tens of thousands of diggers and African migrant workers poured in. Claim holders were mostly white, and disputes over labour, permits and illicit diamond buying shaped politics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-rebellion">Black Flag Rebellion</a> of white diggers in 1875 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-southey">Southey</a>'s recall. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/batlhaping-chiefdom">Tlhaping</a> rising in 1878 was suppressed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The colony was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1880. Its diamond fields became the base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-beers">De Beers</a> company, and the methods used there, such as closed labour compounds, shaped South African mining.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1873 CE – 1880 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.98%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1873 CE – 1880 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kimberley</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Richard Southey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Dutch, Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crown Colony of Griqualand West</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=griqualand-west-colony" aria-label="Griqualand West Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5327 3229 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Griqualand West Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5647 3485L5647 3485M5647 3485L4997 651M5647 3485L5470 3663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5647 3485h0M4997 651h0M5470 3663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 3485h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 3485h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kimberley. <a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=griqualand-west-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
